{"schema_version":"trial-prediction-json-v1","trial_id":"NCT04630145","knowledge_cutoff_post":true,"analysis_trace":["Trial NCT04630145 is a Phase 3 study of bedaquiline combined with clarithromycin and ethambutol for treatment-refractory MAC-LD. Knowledge cutoff is 2026-01-31. Registry lookup returned HTTP 403, so design details are inferred from the provided contract. The trial tests bedaquiline (a diarylquinoline ATP synthase inhibitor approved for MDR-TB) repurposed for MAC-LD. Primary endpoint is sputum culture conversion in MGIT at Week 24. Secondary endpoints include culture conversion on solid media (7H10/7H11), patient-reported outcomes (SGRQ), time-to-event endpoints, and safety.","Evidence search retrieved literature on MAC-LD treatment and bedaquiline use. Key findings: (1) Bedaquiline shows in vitro activity against M. avium complex (MAC) via ATP synthase inhibition, with MICs typically 0.06-0.5 mg/L; (2) Case series (2025) report bedaquiline + clofazimine salvage therapy achieving culture conversion in refractory NTM-LD; (3) Standard MAC-LD therapy (clarithromycin + ethambutol ± rifamycin) achieves culture conversion in 60-80% of treatment-naive patients at 6 months, but refractory disease has lower response rates (~30-50%); (4) Bedaquiline's QT prolongation risk is well-documented from TB trials.","Evidence quality assessment: No direct Phase 3 results for this specific regimen were found before cutoff. Indirect evidence from bedaquiline mechanism, MAC-LD natural history, and salvage therapy case series supports plausible efficacy. The addition of bedaquiline to standard backbone therapy (clarithromycin + ethambutol) is mechanistically rational. Primary endpoint prediction relies on: (a) bedaquiline's demonstrated anti-MAC activity, (b) standard therapy partial efficacy in refractory disease, (c) Phase 3 design implying sponsor confidence.","Population: Treatment-refractory MAC-LD patients have failed prior standard therapy, so baseline conversion rates are lower than naive patients. Estimand likely ITT or per-protocol with microbiological endpoint.","Safety: Bedaquiline's known QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity, and drug-drug interactions with clarithromycin (CYP3A4) are key risks. ECG monitoring is expected per label.","Key uncertainties: (1) No published Phase 3 results before cutoff; (2) Exact refractory population definition unknown; (3) Concomitant rifamycin use (rifampicin/rifabutin) creates drug interaction complexity; (4) Duration of bedaquiline exposure in MAC-LD unclear.","Forecast approach: For primary endpoint (culture conversion rate at Week 24), estimate based on: standard refractory MAC therapy ~30-40% historical conversion; bedaquiline addition may improve to 45-60% based on mechanism and salvage case reports. Confidence moderate-low due to indirect evidence.","For secondary endpoints: Culture conversion on solid media typically correlates with MGIT but may be slightly lower (40-55%). SGRQ improvement in MAC-LD with effective therapy typically shows 4-8 point mean reduction. Time-to-conversion median estimate 8-16 weeks based on TB bedaquiline experience extrapolated. Safety endpoints expected to show QT prolongation in 5-15% and hepatotoxicity in 5-10%."],"search_verification":[{"url":"https://europepmc.org/article/MED/41416824","supports":"Bedaquiline-clofazimine combination evaluated for macrolide-resistant MAC infection, supporting mechanism rationale","date":"2025-12-19"},{"url":"https://europepmc.org/article/MED/41023876","supports":"Bedaquiline + clofazimine salvage therapy for NTM-LD, 11 patients, culture conversion achieved","date":"2025-09-29"},{"url":"https://europepmc.org/article/MED/36609820","supports":"Contemporary pharmacotherapies for NTM infections, clarithromycin-ethambutol backbone efficacy data","date":"2023-01-07"},{"url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bcr-2025-268609","supports":"Bedaquiline safety signal in MAC-LD (eosinophilic pneumonitis case)","date":"2025-11-30"},{"url":"https://europepmc.org/article/MED/41209286","supports":"Bedaquiline case report for NTM-PD after treatment failure","date":"2025-10-17"}],"endpoint_predictions":[{"endpoint_id":"primary-0","prediction_status":"predicted","value":{"endpoint_met_call":"uncertain","probability_endpoint_met":0.55,"effect_measure":"percentage","effect_point":52.5,"ci_low":38.0,"ci_high":67.0,"estimand":"ITT or per-protocol, treatment-refractory MAC-LD patients, Week 24","group_predictions":[{"group_label":"Bedaquiline + Clarithromycin + Ethambutol","predicted_rate":52.5,"denominator":null}],"confidence":0.55,"uncertainty":"No direct Phase 3 results; extrapolated from salvage therapy case series and standard therapy response rates in refractory disease","rationale":"Based on bedaquiline in vitro activity against MAC, salvage case series showing culture conversion, and standard clarithromycin-ethambutol backbone achieving ~30-40% in refractory disease. Bedaquiline addition estimated to improve conversion to 45-60% range. Point estimate 52.5% with wide CI reflecting Phase 3 uncertainty."},"direction":"higher_is_better","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Bedaquiline adds novel mechanism to standard therapy; Phase 3 design suggests sponsor efficacy hypothesis","reason":""},{"endpoint_id":"secondary-1","prediction_status":"predicted","value":{"endpoint_met_call":"uncertain","probability_endpoint_met":0.52,"effect_measure":"percentage","effect_point":48.0,"ci_low":34.0,"ci_high":62.0,"estimand":"ITT or per-protocol, solid media conversion at Week 24","group_predictions":[{"group_label":"Bedaquiline + Clarithromycin + Ethambutol","predicted_rate":48.0,"denominator":null}],"confidence":0.5,"uncertainty":"Solid media conversion typically slightly lower than liquid MGIT; no direct data","rationale":"Solid media (7H10/7H11) culture conversion historically lags MGIT by 5-10 percentage points in MAC studies. Based on primary endpoint estimate of 52.5% MGIT conversion, solid media conversion predicted at 48%."},"direction":"higher_is_better","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Solid media conversion correlates with MGIT but typically lower","reason":""},{"endpoint_id":"secondary-2","prediction_status":"predicted","value":{"endpoint_met_call":"uncertain","probability_endpoint_met":0.6,"effect_measure":"mean_change","effect_point":-6.5,"unit":"points","ci_low":-10.0,"ci_high":-3.0,"estimand":"Change from baseline in SGRQ total score at Week 24","group_predictions":[{"group_label":"Bedaquiline + Clarithromycin + Ethambutol","predicted_mean_change":-6.5,"unit":"points"}],"confidence":0.55,"uncertainty":"Limited MAC-LD specific SGRQ data; extrapolated from COPD and TB treatment response","rationale":"Effective MAC-LD therapy typically associated with 4-8 point SGRQ improvement. Bedaquiline-containing regimen with expected microbiological response predicts 6.5 point mean reduction (improvement), with conservative CI given limited specific data."},"direction":"lower_is_better","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Microbiological response correlates with patient-reported outcome improvement","reason":""},{"endpoint_id":"secondary-3","prediction_status":"predicted","value":{"endpoint_met_call":"uncertain","probability_endpoint_met":0.58,"effect_measure":"percentage","effect_point":65.0,"ci_low":50.0,"ci_high":78.0,"estimand":"Culture conversion at Week 48 and Week 60 in MGIT and solid media","group_predictions":[{"group_label":"Bedaquiline + Clarithromycin + Ethambutol","predicted_rate":65.0,"denominator":null}],"confidence":0.55,"uncertainty":"Longer follow-up increases conversion rates but bedaquiline exposure duration unclear","rationale":"Extended treatment duration typically increases culture conversion rates in MAC-LD. Week 48/60 conversion predicted higher than Week 24 (65% vs 52.5%) given additional treatment time, assuming bedaquiline continued or completed per protocol."},"direction":"higher_is_better","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Longer treatment duration allows more time for culture conversion","reason":""},{"endpoint_id":"secondary-4","prediction_status":"predicted","value":{"endpoint_met_call":"uncertain","probability_endpoint_met":0.5,"effect_measure":"percentage","effect_point":45.0,"ci_low":30.0,"ci_high":60.0,"estimand":"Sustained culture negativity from Week 2 through Week 60","group_predictions":[{"group_label":"Bedaquiline + Clarithromycin + Ethambutol","predicted_rate":45.0,"denominator":null}],"confidence":0.45,"uncertainty":"Sustained negativity is stricter endpoint than conversion; includes risk of reversion","rationale":"Sustained negativity requires maintaining negative cultures after initial conversion. Historical MAC-LD reversion rates 15-25% predict lower sustained negativity than simple conversion. Estimate 45% sustained negativity from Week 2-60."},"direction":"higher_is_better","confidence":0.45,"rationale":"Sustained negativity is harder endpoint; reversion risk reduces rate vs conversion","reason":""},{"endpoint_id":"secondary-5","prediction_status":"predicted","value":{"endpoint_met_call":"uncertain","probability_endpoint_met":0.55,"effect_measure":"median_time_to_event","effect_point":12.0,"unit":"weeks","ci_low":8.0,"ci_high":20.0,"estimand":"Median time to sputum culture conversion in MGIT up to Week 48","group_predictions":[{"group_label":"Bedaquiline + Clarithromycin + Ethambutol","median_time_weeks":12.0}],"confidence":0.5,"uncertainty":"Extrapolated from TB bedaquiline experience; MAC-LD kinetics may differ","rationale":"In MDR-TB, bedaquiline reduces time to culture conversion with median ~8-12 weeks. MAC-LD may respond slower due to indolent course and refractory population. Predicted median 12 weeks (range 8-20) for conversion."},"direction":"lower_is_better","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Faster conversion indicates treatment efficacy","reason":""}],"trial_outcome_prediction":{"classification":"uncertain","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Phase 3 design with novel bedaquiline addition to standard MAC therapy is mechanistically rational and supported by salvage case series, but no direct efficacy results available before cutoff. Success probability moderate given sponsor investment and plausible mechanism, but uncertainty remains for refractory population response rates."}}

{"schema_version":"trial-prediction-json-v1","trial_id":"NCT04630145","analysis_trace":["Trial NCT04630145 is a Phase 3 study of bedaquiline combined with clarithromycin and ethambutol for treatment-refractory MAC-LD. The trial design includes multiple treatment arms with bedaquiline, clarithromycin, ethambutol, rifampicin, and rifabutin.","Evidence search found limited direct data on this specific combination for MAC-LD. Bedaquiline is approved for MDR-TB but off-label for NTM. Macrolide (clarithromycin) + ethambutol is standard therapy for MAC-LD.","For secondary-6 (Time to Positivity in MGIT): This is a microbiological endpoint measuring time to culture positivity. Limited data on bedaquiline in MAC-LD; extrapolated from MDR-TB experience where bedaquiline shows early bactericidal activity. Predicted median time to positivity reduction of 20-30% vs standard therapy.","For secondary-7 (SGRQ change at Weeks 48/60): Quality of life improvement expected with effective MAC treatment. Based on MAC-LD studies showing SGRQ improvements of 5-10 points with successful therapy. Predicted mean change of -6 points (improvement).","For secondary-8, secondary-9, secondary-10 (Lung function parameters): These endpoints are marked as not_evaluable in the contract due to insufficient validation in MAC-LD trials. Spirometry changes in MAC-LD are variable and not well-correlated with microbiological outcomes.","For secondary-11 (Treatment regimen change): This measures treatment modification rates due to efficacy or safety. In refractory MAC-LD, regimen changes are common. Predicted 30-40% change rate by Week 24, 50-60% by Week 48/60.","Safety considerations: Bedaquiline has known QT prolongation risk; clarithromycin also prolongs QT. Combination requires monitoring. Historical data shows bedaquiline AE rate ~60-70% in TB studies."],"search_verification":[{"url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/jcph.1887","claim":"Population pharmacokinetic analysis of bedaquiline-clarithromycin combination